Follow-up: default release nested smoke PASS after removing the stale socket
created by the nested lock test; opt-in X11 mapping/popup/IPC-close smoke PASS.
The 4/4 clipboard and primary text transfers passed again. While the first
X11 owner remained alive, a Wayland client took ownership and X11 fetched the
new text, for both clipboard and primary (2/2 PASS). Nested swaylock again
denied a new X11 read of a Wayland-owned clipboard (None). The lock test's
test-created IPC socket was removed after confirming no process/listener was
using it. No direct-hardware or xterm-visible lock test was performed here.

Controlled nested timing harness (`scripts/smoke_xwayland_selection_lock.py`
with `examples/selection_probe.rs` and a GTK3 Python selection owner):
`old-offer` PASSed twice — an X11 clipboard offer granted before locking
delivered zero bytes when its `receive` was issued after a nested locker
confirmed the lock; this is the first reproducible evidence that Wisp denies
that post-lock path rather than relying on the single ordinary request seen
before. `in-flight` is a known diagnostic failure: a large in-flight
X11->Wayland transfer continued after lock confirmation (7,073,796 bytes in
one run before the harness was made informational). Cause: Smithay 0.7's
XWM pumps already-started receives with no public cancel API; canceling
mid-flight would require upstream support or vendoring the XWM. New receives
remain denied while locked. The harness reports a leak count, does not claim
lock security, and reruns are nested-only. Direct hardware and direct-VT X11
behavior remain outside this harness.

User-led nested xterm check: the screenshot shows `wl-copy` in foot followed
by `xclip -selection clip` read in xterm returning the Wayland text, then
